latin1_comment: 1:15: a Zed file is UTF-8 text, and the bytes here, E9 in hexadecimal, are not a UTF-8 character, so the file cannot be read. Save the file as UTF-8 in your editor, or replace these bytes with the character they were meant to be. [bytes 14..15]
decomposed_name: 2:12: these characters are not in Unicode Normalization Form C (NFC), which spells them as U+00E9. A Zed file must be in NFC, so that two names that look the same are the same name. Retype the characters, or have your editor save the file in NFC. [bytes 33..36]
byte_order_mark: 1:1: the file starts with a byte order mark (the bytes EF BB BF), and a Zed file may not, since the same program would then be stored in two ways. Save the file as UTF-8 without a byte order mark (in many editors this is "UTF-8" rather than "UTF-8 with BOM"). [bytes 0..3]
truncated_download: 1:9: a Zed file is UTF-8 text, and the bytes here, E2 82 in hexadecimal, are not a UTF-8 character, so the file cannot be read. Save the file as UTF-8 in your editor, or replace these bytes with the character they were meant to be. [bytes 8..10]
